@page "/projects/blazor-app/ui"
@page "/projects/game-development/infiniroller"

<div class="project-page page-content">
	<header class="project-header">
		<p class="eyebrow">Game Development</p>
		<h1>Infiniroller</h1>
		<p class="tagline">A published Unity iOS game released to the App Store in March 2025.</p>
	</header>

	<section class="project-section">
		<h2>Published Game</h2>
		<p>
			In March 2025, I released <strong>Infiniroller</strong> to the Apple App Store. The project represents full-cycle independent game delivery: Unity implementation, C# gameplay work, mobile polish, platform compliance, App Store submission, and release ownership.
		</p>
		<p>
			This is the kind of project I value because it forces decisions all the way down. A game is not finished when the prototype works; it is finished when it can survive real device constraints, store requirements, player-facing polish, and the unglamorous edges of release.
		</p>
		<p>
			<a class="project-link" href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/infiniroller/id6743130001" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
				<i class="fa-brands fa-app-store-ios" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<span>View Infiniroller on the App Store</span>
			</a>
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="project-section">
		<h2>What It Demonstrates</h2>
		<ul class="project-list">
			<li>Unity and C# gameplay implementation.</li>
			<li>Mobile-focused iteration and release discipline.</li>
			<li>Independent ownership across design, build, testing, and publication.</li>
			<li>Comfort moving from creative prototype to shipped product.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section class="project-section">
		<h2>Earlier Work</h2>
		<p>
			I also have additional portfolio projects from my KSU game development days. Those projects are part of the foundation, but Infiniroller is the clearest published marker of my current independent game development work.
		</p>
	</section>
</div>
